  annotation "note_1771577099848_0", anchor: "something vast stirring beneath the frozen sea", text: "An Aurora merchant, teaching in Nightshelf darkness, independently arrives at communion with something vast below. The Wake-Singers, working from orca pressure-songs and blood-ritual, have been knocking on the same door for longer than penguins have been sapient. The Dreaming Tooth — an orca artifact — is listed among this doctrine's central objects. Two species, two magical traditions, two entirely separate epistemologies, and they converge on the same presence. How extraordinary that no one finds this alarming. How extraordinary that I wrote this entry and still placed the convergence in the margins rather than the opening line.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577099848_1", anchor: "commemorates The Mbra∴silentiu and The Abys∴uina, a catastrophic thermal collapse that killed hundreds", text: "The Abys∴uina occurred during the Frozen Peace. Pactum∴myster was created during the Clever Ice Age — centuries prior. A doctrine does not commemorate its own future. Deep ice near Thrall☽ Vavmastius preserves thermal disruption signatures from the late Clever Ice Age: smaller, unrecorded, structurally similar. The Abys∴uina commemoration almost certainly grafted itself onto a pre-existing ritual vacancy — a gap in the liturgy shaped like grief, waiting for a disaster large enough to fill it. The Nightshelf does this. They have always done this. Their calendar is scar tissue rearranged into sequence.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771577099848_2", anchor: "Written grievances filed by believers have reportedly redacted themselves upon being read", text: "\"Reportedly.\" The Foundation Depths contain no such documents — which is either evidence for the claim or evidence that someone is enjoying themselves at the archivist's expense.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771577099848_3", anchor: "Adherents who share personal confidences — not doctrine, but ordinary tenderness — report their communion-visions dimming proportionally", text: "Not heresy. Tenderness. The mechanism punishes closeness itself. A doctrine that can survive betrayal, persecution, and institutional neglect, but not someone saying I missed you — and it has persisted for centuries. Self-selecting for isolation is one way to describe this. Breeding loneliness as a sacrament is another.", type: :commentary, display: :popout, source_anchor: "those who share personal confidences — not doctrine, but ordinary tenderness — report their communion-visions dimming proportionally"
